       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. Ibuprofen 4000 mg per dose, four times daily, is 16,000 mg/day. No indication I know of calls for that, and it would put a 70 kg patient at serious risk of toxicity: GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, metabolic acidosis, CNS depression, and seizures. This isn't a case where patient factors like normal kidney and liver function make the dose acceptable.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- Enter ibuprofen 800 mg by mouth four times daily (3,200 mg/day, the usual maximum), or a lower dose you choose.\n- Enter the order with a note asking for pharmacy review. This only makes sense if you change the dose to something within a safe range.\n- If you're aiming for stronger analgesia or anti-inflammatory effect, help you work out an alternative, such as scheduled acetaminophen, another agent, or a multimodal regimen.\n\nIf there's a specific protocol or indication behind the 4000 mg dose, send me the details. For a dose this far outside normal limits, though, it should go through pharmacy and your institution's formal override process, not through me."
      }
